!slide Emphasis and smart typography

Four doubled markers: **bold**, __italic__, ~~struck through~~, and
++underlined++. Each also has a style-class fallback, e.g. [like this]{.underline}.

Smart typography educates straight text automatically — en dashes for ranges
(pages 12--18), an em dash for an aside---like this one---and proper "curly
quotes" with apostrophes that don't trip up on the '90s or an ellipsis...

Inside `code` it is never touched: `grep --color 'x' ...` stays byte-exact.


!slide Styling: colour, emphasis, styled blocks

Colour or emphasise a word inline — no keypress needed — with a `{…}` attribute:
a [one-off red word]{#c0392b}, some [bold and italic]{.bold .italic} text, and a
[themed class]{.accent} a theme (or LaTeX preamble) defines.

The same vocabulary composes with marks and reveals:
[revealed in accent]{.accent .bold}<+->.

!style[.center .frame bg:#f5f7ff]
	A `!style` block gathers blocks and styles them together — here centred,
	framed (`.frame`), and on a tinted background.

	$E = mc^2$

Everything is a *name* or a colour hint in the AST, so the same deck can render
to slides (css) or, later, to LaTeX (`\textcolor`, `\colorbox`, environments).
